Description: antinomie: hold an exiting process of a dump-trigger run until the host has judged it
 The host's dump trigger judges each call as it arrives and asks for a
 core of the process that made a suspicious one. On an idle host that
 takes about a millisecond, under load a hundred or more: a process that
 exits a millisecond after writing its cron entry is gone before the
 request comes, and its core is lost.
 .
 A run can now be armed for an exit hold: EXEC flag EXIT_HOLD at the
 launch, or the EXIT_HOLD command's ARM for a running run. A process of
 an armed run that ends (exit_group, the exit of its last thread, a
 fatal or core-dumping signal) first publishes lifecycle.exit_hold and
 then waits, at the start of its exit and before any of it is torn down,
 for at most 50 ms. The host asks for its core as for any live process
 (CORE_CAPTURE by its identity): the process then writes the core itself
 with the exit core's writer, into a live handle flagged EXIT_HELD, and
 goes on exiting. Or the host lets it go (EXIT_HOLD RELEASE), which is
 what it does at once for a process no rule matched. SIGKILL, a process
 whose own exit core is armed, a stopped run (STOP_RUN) and the timeout
 end the wait; a process is held once, and at most eight wait at a time.
 .
 The hold sits in the process core's exit hook, ant_monitor_core_exit(),
 which every way a process ends already reaches (do_group_exit(), the
 last thread's do_exit(), do_coredump()): no stock kernel file changes.
 exit_hold.c keeps the held processes; manual_core.c hands a held
 process's CORE_CAPTURE to it; process_core.c makes and writes the held
 core; run.c keeps the run's arming; control.c takes the flag and the
 command; state.h marks a process as offered.
 .
 Contract: revision 22. The flag and the command are offered in
 TRACER_CAPS; an older guest rejects both.
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-10

